title: Dust accounting in fungibles transfers and approvals

doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet_assets::prep_debit` promotes a debit to the account's whole balance when debiting the
    requested amount exactly would strand a remainder below the asset's `min_balance`. Two
    consumers assumed the debit equalled the amount they asked for, and each lost track of the
    difference.

    The default `fungible::Mutate::transfer` and `fungibles::Mutate::transfer` pair a `BestEffort`
    `decrease_balance` with an `increase_balance` of `amount`, discarding the figure actually
    debited. Where the two differ the remainder left the source without being credited to anyone
    or deducted from the supply, since neither `Unbalanced` method adjusts it — leaving total
    issuance permanently above the sum of all balances. Both defaults now take the figure
    `decrease_balance` returned for the credit, the return value and the `Transferred` event,
    the last of which is what indexers read. Both defaults also debit with `Exact` instead of
    `BestEffort`, so a `decrease_balance` that cannot take the full `amount` fails the transfer
    rather than moving less, and the credit's error is propagated where it used to be discarded,
    so a credit that does not fit fails the transfer instead of stranding the debit. A zero
    transfer returns early without touching either account, as a self-transfer already did.

    Porting notes. An `Unbalanced::decrease_balance` implementation must do one of two things with
    a sub-minimum remainder, never both: return exactly `amount` and pass the remainder to
    `handle_dust`, as `pallet-balances` does, or fold it into the return value and keep it in the
    system, as `pallet-assets` and the `ItemOf` and `UnionOf` adapters over it do. A caller
    crediting the return value would otherwise issue the remainder a second time. On the calling
    side, `Mutate::transfer` and `done_transfer` can now report more than `amount`, by up to
    `minimum_balance() - 1`; callers that book the requested figure should read the returned one.

    `do_transfer_approved` measured the approval against the requested amount and then moved the
    promoted debit, so a delegate could move up to `min_balance - 1` more than it was approved
    for, to a destination of its own choosing, while only the requested amount was charged
    against the allowance. It now resolves the debit first and measures the approval against
    that, leaving the approval untouched when it does not fit. Where the approval does cover the
    promoted debit the transfer still goes through, but it is now the debit that is charged, so
    an approval can be exhausted — and its entry removed and its deposit returned — by a request
    smaller than the approval. A self-transfer moves nothing, so the promotion no longer applies
    to it: it is charged, and reported in `Transferred`, as the requested amount.

- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    An approved transfer that would leave the owner holding a non-zero balance below the asset's
    minimum now fails with `Unapproved` unless the approval covers the owner's whole balance,
    rather than moving more than was approved. A delegate can request up to the owner's balance
    less the minimum; a larger request moves the owner's whole balance and needs an approval
    covering it, and spends that whole balance out of the approval.

    The same applies to the ERC-20 `transferFrom` of the assets precompile: a call whose value is
    within both the allowance and the owner's balance now reverts when the owner's remainder would
    fall below the asset's minimum and the allowance does not cover the whole balance.

crates:
- name: frame-support
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-assets
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: none
